<DocsLayout
  title="Security"
  description="Understand the trust model, privileged engine authority, secret handling, network boundaries, and owner responsibilities."
>
  <section>
    <p class="overline">Trust model</p>
    <h2>One host, a trusted operator group, explicit privileged surfaces</h2>
    <p>
      MyPaaS is a single-host control plane for one owner or a small trusted team. The API controls the configured
      Docker-compatible engine socket, which is effectively host-level container-engine authority. Treat compromise
      of the MyPaaS API boundary as compromise of the MyPaaS host boundary.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fresh supported hosts are Podman-first, but the control plane deliberately keeps the same Docker-compatible
      command/socket contract when rootful Podman is the engine. Docker Engine remains an explicit compatibility mode.
      This trust model does not provide VM-grade or hostile multi-tenant isolation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Control summary</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Boundary</th><th>Control</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Dashboard access</strong></td><td>GitHub OAuth plus the instance authorization model; the platform targets an owner or small trusted team.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Project environment</strong></td><td>Persisted project environment values are encrypted before storage.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>GitHub webhook</strong></td><td>Per-project secret with HMAC verification and branch filtering.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Engine authority</strong></td><td>The API receives the Docker-compatible engine socket; project workloads do not receive engine authority.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Caddy administration</strong></td><td>Production Caddy Admin is Unix-socket only; project workloads do not receive the admin socket.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Runtime routing</strong></td><td>Only explicitly routed runtimes join the routing network; Caddy reaches them through the managed routing path.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Compose input</strong></td><td>Rendered Compose configuration is checked against platform isolation policy before execution; known host-escape features are rejected.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Database writes</strong></td><td>DB Studio is read-only by default and write access requires an explicit expiring write session.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Audit trail</strong></td><td>Administrative actions are recorded by the platform audit path.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Network separation</h2>
    <p>
      Production uses distinct control, project, and routing networks. The API, dashboard, and cloudflared stay on the
      control network; the API does not join project or routing networks. Caddy does not join the general project
      network, and a project runtime gains routing-network membership only when its route is explicitly activated.
    </p>
    <p>
      An allocated host port is used as a stable runtime lookup key. The normal production Caddy data path resolves the
      validated runtime and proxies over the routing network rather than treating an arbitrary host address as a fallback.
      Route resolution is fail-closed when the expected runtime cannot be validated.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Secrets and environment values</h2>
    <p>
      Project environment values are encrypted before persistence and are decrypted only for authenticated operations
      that need them. Back up the MyPaaS instance configuration and encryption material with the same care as the
      control-plane database; encrypted values are not useful without the corresponding key material.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not paste secrets into issue trackers, deployment names, repository files, screenshots, or logs. Rotate GitHub
      OAuth, webhook, JWT, database, and Cloudflare credentials after suspected exposure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Compose is not a host escape hatch</h2>
    <p>
      Repository Compose files are rendered and evaluated before execution. The current policy rejects privileged mode,
      host or container namespace sharing, Docker/Podman socket mounts, host bind mounts, devices and GPUs, added Linux
      capabilities, custom runtimes, external networks or volumes, unsafe build entitlements, build SSH/secrets, and
      privileged lifecycle hooks. Safe engine-managed named volumes are handled separately from engine-migration portability.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Public exposure</h2>
    <p>
      The intended production path is Internet → Cloudflare Tunnel → Caddy → dashboard, API, static release, or an
      explicitly routed runtime. Caddy's privileged Admin API is not exposed on production TCP port 2019; it uses the
      MyPaaS Unix-socket control path instead.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Owner responsibilities</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Restrict SSH and operating-system administration access.</li>
      <li>Keep the VM, selected container engine, MyPaaS release, and supporting system packages updated deliberately.</li>
      <li>Verify scheduled backups and retain additional off-host copies appropriate to the workload.</li>
      <li>Review repository Dockerfiles and Compose files even though MyPaaS applies its own Compose policy checks.</li>
      <li>Monitor host disk, memory, CPU, network, and Cloudflare configuration.</li>
      <li>Remove collaborators and rotate credentials when access is no longer required.</li>
    </ul>
    <aside class="warning"><p>Self-hosting preserves infrastructure ownership; it does not turn a single-host container platform into a hostile multi-tenant security boundary.</p></aside>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>